# Gas Turbine Brayton Cycle Model

**Project Type:** Propulsion Engineering / Thermodynamic Analysis

## Overview

This project models the ideal Brayton cycle for a gas turbine, analysing 
how pressure ratio affects thermal efficiency and specific net work output. 
The model calculates temperature and work at each stage of the cycle 
(compression, heat addition, expansion) and sweeps pressure ratio across a 
realistic range (5–40) to identify performance trends relevant to real 
turbofan engine design.

## Key Outcomes

- **Efficiency Trend:** Thermal efficiency rises from ~37% at a pressure 
  ratio of 5 to ~65% at a pressure ratio of 40, with diminishing returns 
  at higher pressure ratios — consistent with the ideal cycle efficiency 
  relation η = 1 - (1/rp)^((γ-1)/γ).
- **Specific Work Trade-off:** Specific net work peaks at ~476 kJ/kg 
  around a pressure ratio of ~18–20, then declines at higher pressure 
  ratios. This occurs because compressor work grows faster than turbine 
  work at high pressure ratios, illustrating why real gas turbines don't 
  simply maximise pressure ratio.
- **Validation:** Results were checked against the analytical ideal-cycle 
  efficiency formula to confirm the model's equations are correct.

## Method

The model uses isentropic compression/expansion relations to calculate 
temperature at each of the four cycle stations (compressor inlet, 
compressor outlet, turbine inlet, turbine outlet), then derives compressor 
work, turbine work, net work, heat input, and thermal efficiency from 
these temperatures. A cold-air-standard assumption is used (constant 
specific heat cp = 1005 J/kg·K and γ = 1.4).

## Technologies Used

- **MATLAB:** Vectorised computation across a pressure ratio sweep, 
  and visualisation using subplots.

## Possible Extensions

- Sweep turbine inlet temperature to show its effect on specific work.
- Add compressor and turbine isentropic efficiencies to model a real 
  (non-ideal) cycle and compare against the ideal case.
